<!-- =============================== TIMELINE =============================== -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-a-line-through-the-subject">A Line Through the Subject</h2>
    <ul class="timeline">
      <li><strong>1917</strong> &mdash; Einstein introduces the cosmological constant, and later regrets it prematurely. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1933</strong> &mdash; Zwicky weighs the Coma cluster and finds most of it missing. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1970s</strong> &mdash; Rubin and Ford measure flat rotation curves galaxy after galaxy. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1998</strong> &mdash; Two supernova teams find the expansion accelerating; Nobel in 2011. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1999</strong> &mdash; Webb and colleagues first report a varying fine-structure constant; still unconfirmed. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>2003&ndash;2009</strong> &mdash; Zurek's quantum Darwinism: objectivity as redundancy in the environment. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>2006</strong> &mdash; The Bullet Cluster separates mass from gas, and the case for dark matter hardens. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>2012</strong> &mdash; Landauer's bound measured directly in a single colloidal bit. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>2018</strong> &mdash; Planck's final release fixes the three fractions to the figures used on this page. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>2024&ndash;25</strong> &mdash; DESI reports a mild preference for evolving dark energy; the field watches. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>In progress</strong> &mdash; The Two Infinities Framework, James Rankin: the Seam, the Ledger and the Skeletal Splice set out together, with the mathematics tested. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

